00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:49.720
Most cloud providers use what's
called hot potato routing.

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:51.730
This is when you're
sending out traffic

00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:53.920
from their infrastructure,
they offload it

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:56.360
to the public internet
as soon as possible.

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:58.000
This is like a hot
potato that they

00:01:58.000 --> 00:01:59.920
want to get off their hands.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:01.900
Google Cloud as well
provides this option

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:04.960
in the standard networking
tier, but we also

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:06.520
do things differently.

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:08.770
In Google Cloud's
premium networking tier,

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:11.950
we use something called the
cold potato routing technique,

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:15.400
where we keep that traffic on
Google Cloud's private network

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:17.620
for as long as
possible and exit it

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:20.890
to the public internet at the
location closest to the end

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:23.980
user, usually in the
same town or city.

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:27.730
This enables us to offer you
with better reliability, better

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:29.980
performance, and more security.

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:32.770
And this is possible due to
the Google's global network

00:02:32.770 --> 00:02:35.260
we've been talking
about, the big benefits

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:38.770
being high performance, reduced
latency, and superior network

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:42.790
quality, leading to a
delightful user experience.

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:45.850
Hence, the Premium Tier is the
recommended and default tier,

00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:49.240
