00:02:59.930 --> 00:03:03.160
DEOK FILHO: Coming up
next, VPC networking.

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:07.870
A VPC, or Virtual Private Cloud,
is your virtual global network

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:08.980
in Google Cloud.

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:11.080
As you can see from
this image, they

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:14.050
are created within
projects and are made up

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:16.690
of subnets, which are regional.

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:18.760
VPCs enable private
communication

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:21.460
between Google Cloud
regions natively,

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:23.560
allowing you to keep
a fault tolerant

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:27.100
in highly available
network by default.

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:30.160
When creating a VPC in
the Google Cloud Console

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:33.580
or through the Cloud SDK,
you can choose from two

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:35.920
custom subnet creation modes--

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:39.610
auto subnet mode, which
creates a subnet for each

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:41.830
of Google Cloud's
existing regions,

00:03:41.830 --> 00:03:44.320
or custom subnet
mode, which allows

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:47.590
you to create a subnet for
each of the regions you'd like.

00:03:47.590 --> 00:03:50.020
Creating a VPC with
custom subnet mode

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:53.360
is our recommended
best practice.

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:56.530
Let's take a look at
how to create a VPC.

00:03:56.530 --> 00:03:58.960
Log on to the Google
Cloud Console.

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:04.210
Select the list view of Google
products, and open VPC Network.

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:06.640
Notice that there is
already a network created

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:10.780
called "default," which
spans all regions and is

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:13.600
of type auto subnet mode.

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:18.510
Let's now create a new network
using custom subnet mode.

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:21.010
Select Create VPC Network.

00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:24.660
Give it an identifiable name,
making sure to select Custom

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:27.450
under Subnet Creation Mode.

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:30.300
In the new subnet box,
you'll be able to create

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:32.130
multiple subnets.

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:34.140
First, choose a name.

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:36.180
Select a region
from the dropdown.

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:40.750
And choose a private IP
range for this subnet.

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:43.570
Turn private Google
access on if you

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:47.890
wish to allow VMs in this subnet
to communicate with Google APIs

00:04:47.890 --> 00:04:50.290
without needing external IPs.

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:54.400
Then, toggle Flow Logs On if
you'd like to see detailed

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:56.710
logs for your network traffic.

00:04:56.710 --> 00:05:00.940
Notice that toggling this
on will incur extra costs.

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:03.190
Repeat these steps
for all the subnets

00:05:03.190 --> 00:05:05.920
you wish to create in this VPC.

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:08.800
Finally, choose
Global if you need

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:10.870
to make your newly
created subnets

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:13.330
available to your
on-prem network.

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:16.750
If not, leave Regional selected.

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:18.430
Click Create.

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:22.060
Your VPC will be
provisioned within seconds.

00:05:22.060 --> 00:05:24.460
Let's talk a bit
about firewall rules.

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:27.880
Unlike a traditional firewall,
which is a physical appliance

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:30.610
filtering traffic
between subnets,

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:34.000
Google Cloud's firewall rules
provide distributed enforcement

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:36.920
at the virtual networking level.

00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:38.680
This means that there
is no performance

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:40.360
bottleneck or
[? singular ?] pointer

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:43.660
failure in using our firewall.

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:46.630
Firewall rules are
included at no extra cost,

00:05:46.630 --> 00:05:51.520
and there are no appliances or
operating systems to manage.

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:54.430
Essentially, every
VPC network functions

00:05:54.430 --> 00:05:57.280
as a distributed firewall.

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:00.610
Finally, you can think
of GCP firewall rules

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:03.130
as existing not only
between your instances

00:06:03.130 --> 00:06:07.210
in other networks, but also
between individual instances

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:09.760
within the same network.

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:11.800
When creating a
firewall rule, you

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:14.470
must specify a few
configurations.

00:06:14.470 --> 00:06:16.780
At a basic level,
a firewall rule

00:06:16.780 --> 00:06:19.390
needs a target, which
controls the instances

00:06:19.390 --> 00:06:21.190
to which the rules apply.

00:06:21.190 --> 00:06:25.140
This can be done by specifying
an instance tag, a service

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:27.180
account that's attached
to your instance,

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:31.690
or simply choosing to apply the
rule to all of the instances.

00:06:31.690 --> 00:06:34.050
Next, you must choose
the direction of traffic

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:39.060
the rules apply to,
ingress or egress traffic.

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:42.000
The rule can then block or
allow the predetermined flow

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:43.620
of traffic.

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:47.250
Finally, you must specify
either a source or a destination

00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:49.380
for the rule to
apply to, depending

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:52.410
on which direction
of traffic you chose.

00:06:52.410 --> 00:06:55.830
Let's now take a look at the
creation of a firewall rule.

00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:58.290
Let's create a rule that
blocks all incoming traffic

00:06:58.290 --> 00:07:00.300
from the internet.

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:02.430
Still in the Google
Cloud Console,

00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:06.570
hover over the VPC Network
and select Firewall.

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:10.230
After selecting Create
Firewall Rule, choose a name,

00:07:10.230 --> 00:07:14.850
select which network this rule
applies to, and set a priority.

00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:18.060
Here, a lower number
means higher priority,

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:21.990
where the highest
priority is denoted by 0.

00:07:21.990 --> 00:07:24.630
Because we're trying to
block all incoming traffic

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:27.240
from the internet,
I'll select Ingress

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:32.400
for destination of traffic
and Deny for action on match.

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:35.640
I'll make this rule apply to
all instances in the network.

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:42.450
And in the source IPv4 ranges,
I'll put 0.0.0/0 to represent

00:07:42.450 --> 00:07:46.990
any and all traffic, including
that from the public internet.

00:07:46.990 --> 00:07:50.700
The final rule is ready,
so now select Create.

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:53.160
Another option for the
configuration of a firewall

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:57.000
rule is to edit a VM instance
directly on the Google Cloud

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:00.030
Console to allow HTTP traffic.

00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:02.700
On the Instances
page, click Edit.

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:05.250
Check Allow HTTP traffic.

00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:06.930
Then, hit Save.

00:08:06.930 --> 00:08:09.070
Once the firewall
rule IS configured,

00:08:09.070 --> 00:08:11.940
you'll be able to see it
in the Firewall Rules page,

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:17.430
where its target is
denoted as HTTP-server.

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:20.370
On the Instance page,
you can see a network tag

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:25.020
for this name,
HTTP-server, signifying

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:28.823
the relationship between
the rule and the instance.

00:08:28.823 --> 00:08:30.240
JEEVANA HEGDE: In
Google Cloud, we

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:33.419
offer different flavors
of load balancers.

00:08:33.419 --> 00:08:35.640
On the global side,
depending on the traffic,

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:41.280
we have load balancers for
HTTP/HTTPS, SSL, and TCP.

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:43.919
On the regional side, we
have network load balancer

00:08:43.919 --> 00:08:48.510
and HTTPS a load balancer, both
external and internal facing.

00:08:48.510 --> 00:08:50.850
External load balancer
distribute the traffic

00:08:50.850 --> 00:08:54.450
coming from the internet to
your Google Cloud VPC network.

00:08:54.450 --> 00:08:56.400
On the other hand,
internal load balancer

00:08:56.400 --> 00:08:59.820
distribute traffic to instances
inside your Google Cloud.

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:02.790
If your backends are distributed
across multiple regions,

00:09:02.790 --> 00:09:04.770
go for the global
Cloud load balancing.

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:07.710
If not, you can choose from the
regional Cloud load balancing

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:08.970
services.

00:09:08.970 --> 00:09:10.830
Google Cloud load
balancing is built

00:09:10.830 --> 00:09:13.020
on the same frontend-serving
infrastructure

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:14.280
that powers Google.

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:17.160
It supports 1 million-plus
queries per second,

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:20.190
with high performance
and low latencies.

00:09:20.190 --> 00:09:22.410
The first picture
shows a load balancing

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:24.240
in a traditional
cloud provider, where

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:26.550
both the load balancer
and the instances

00:09:26.550 --> 00:09:28.320
sit in the data center.

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:30.630
The user request
traverses the internet

00:09:30.630 --> 00:09:32.280
and finally makes
its way to the load

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:34.440
balancer that's sitting
in the data center

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:35.910
by a cloud provider.

00:09:35.910 --> 00:09:37.380
As you can see,
the cloud provider

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:40.510
has no control over the user
experience over the internet.

00:09:40.510 --> 00:09:43.410
So there is no way to deliver
low latency or great user

00:09:43.410 --> 00:09:44.650
experience.

00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:47.910
However, it's different with
Google's global load balancing.

00:09:47.910 --> 00:09:51.000
Since the load balancing is
at 80-plus points of presence

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:52.860
around the world,
the user request

00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:55.770
only traverses the internet
for a short period of time

00:09:55.770 --> 00:09:58.710
until it reaches the closest
Google's Point of Presence,

00:09:58.710 --> 00:09:59.550
or PoP.

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:02.220
Beyond that, the load
balancing system and PoPs

00:10:02.220 --> 00:10:05.220
dominate the TCP connection
and delivers the request

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:08.610
to the service over Google's
high-quality global network.

00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:10.500
The benefits are significant.

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:14.040
To name a few, it provides
reduced latency and improved

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:15.970
throughput for your users.

00:10:15.970 --> 00:10:18.960
It also helps in serving
static casual content

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:22.000
from multiple points
near your users.

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:23.610
Now let's go over
a demonstration

